  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to Crohn disease (trait_efo aligns; phenotyping_reported is Crohn’s disease / Crohn’s disease [regional enteritis], including one time-to-event definition). Among them, PGS004254 shows the strongest reported European validation discrimination (AUROC 0.72, 95% CI 0.70–0.73) and provides an effect size (OR 2.18, 95% CI 2.05–2.32), with a direct endpoint (“Crohn’s disease”) and standard covariates (age, sex, PCs). However, the evidence is limited by metric comparability/packaging: the reported AUROC is a full-model AUROC (not clearly PRS-only), while the snpnet models (PGS001330/PGS001331) provide both PRS-only and full-model metrics, making cross-model comparison imperfect from the visible fields. Additionally, PGS004254’s training sample size is much smaller than the snpnet UKB framework models (though sample size is a weak differentiator per the provided domain guidance). These limitations prevent a high-confidence ‘best’ designation despite PGS004254’s stronger reported AUROC."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter) via either nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) or benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-aligned to ‘nodular goiter’ as a standalone disease risk target.

Among the direct nodular-goiter endpoints, PGS005262 is the best-supported from the visible evidence because it reports an explicit discrimination metric (full-model AUROC 0.5933) in its selected validation record and uses a genome-wide PRS-CS construction with very large variant coverage (1,085,173 variants). It also has multi-ancestry evaluation noted (MAE 100%) with a specified selected validation ancestry set.

However, the overall recommendation remains sub-optimal because (i) the reported AUROC is a full-model metric (not PRS-only), with covariates listed as ‘Unknown’, limiting interpretability and comparability; (ii) the PGS001814/PGS002022 models provide only partial correlation (partial-r) rather than AUC/R2, making cross-candidate performance comparison incomplete; and (iii) several sibling models for benign nodular goiter are close in design and reported performance, leaving residual ambiguity."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001536",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches on trait_reported/trait_efo (vitiligo). By endpoint fidelity, PGS000738 (phenotyping_reported: red hair) and PGS000760 (phenotyping_reported: anti-PD-L1 induced hypothyroidism in cancer patients) appear misaligned with vitiligo as a disease endpoint despite the vitiligo label, which is a major limitation per the provided domain guidance prioritizing phenotyping_reported. PGS001536 is the only model whose phenotyping_reported explicitly targets vitiligo (TTE vitiligo), and it reports PRS-comparable discrimination (PGS-only AUROC 0.64193 with CI; R² ~0.016), with evaluation across multiple ancestries (though selected validation ancestry is European). Confidence is moderate rather than high because the endpoint is time-to-event (may not match a generic case-control vitiligo deployment without additional context) and because the other two models lack comparable PRS-only performance metrics rather than being clearly inferior on reported vitiligo-specific validation."
}
